---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import Draft from '../../components/Draft.astro';
import Provider from '../../components/Provider.astro';
import { entity } from '../../lib/entity';

// Every item below is a row of the service's schema or a line of its code,
// with the real figures: the sweep's days, the cookies' lives, the logs'
// retention as Cloudflare states it. A sentence that cannot be traced to one
// does not go on this page.
const changed = '2026-11-01';
---

<StarlightPage
	frontmatter={{ title: 'Privacy', description: 'What version.cam keeps about you, where, for how long, and why.' }}
>
	<Draft />
	<p class="vc-lead">
		This page says what the hosted service keeps about you, item by item, where it is, how long it stays and
		why it is allowed to. It covers version.cam, app.version.cam, api.version.cam and clips.version.cam. The
		controller of the personal data it describes is:
	</p>
	<Provider role="Controller" />
	<p>
		The versioncam package and the GitHub Action are a different matter: they run in your own CI and on your own
		machines, with your app and your secrets, and send nothing to us until <code>versioncam publish</code> runs.
		Then they send the recordings described below, and nothing else.
	</p>

	<h2>What we keep, and why</h2>
	<p>
		Each item names its legal basis in the words of the GDPR: <em>contract</em> is Article 6(1)(b), the service you
		asked for; <em>legitimate interest</em> is Article 6(1)(f), running and securing the service; <em>legal
		obligation</em> is Article 6(1)(c), tax law. "Kept" is how long, and what removes it.
	</p>
	<dl class="vc-kept not-content">
		<div>
			<dt>Your GitHub identity</dt>
			<dd>
				Your GitHub id, your login and, if GitHub shares one, your public e-mail address (usually none). The
				sign-in asks GitHub for no scope: it learns who you are and nothing you have written. To sign you in and
				to name your account.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Contract · while your account exists</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>What you may reach</dt>
			<dd>
				Which GitHub accounts you belong to where the Versioncam App is installed and, for each of their
				repositories, whether you may read it or administer it, as GitHub lists them. Read at every sign-in and
				replaced whole, so what GitHub no longer grants goes with it. This decides who sees a project and who
				may change it.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Contract and security · replaced at each sign-in</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Your session</dt>
			<dd>
				A session record and the cookie that names it, <code>__Host-vc_session</code>, for seven days. While
				you sign in, a second cookie, <code>__Host-vc_oauth</code>, and the page to return to, for ten minutes.
				Signing out ends the session at once; "sign out everywhere" ends every session and every command-line
				token of yours.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Contract · seven days; the sign-in's state ten minutes</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Command-line tokens</dt>
			<dd>
				A token <code>versioncam cloud login</code> makes once you approve its code in the browser, kept as a
				hash: the service recognises the token and cannot reproduce it.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">
				Contract · until you revoke it or sign out everywhere; an expired one is removed thirty days later
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Your account</dt>
			<dd>
				The account's login, its plan, a plan given by hand and when it ends, whether it is suspended and why,
				and once payment opens, the billing e-mail address and Stripe's id for the customer.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">
				Contract · while the account exists; invoices ten years after the end of the year they belong to
				(ZDDV-1, Article 86)
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Projects</dt>
			<dd>
				A project's repository, its visibility (public, unlisted or private), how many days of versions it
				keeps, the address that gets its notifications, the Slack webhook you give it, and whether it opens
				GitHub issues. A notification address is held as pending until its owner clicks the link we send, and
				we keep when the link went out and when it was confirmed; mail goes only to a confirmed address. The
				Slack webhook is encrypted at rest.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Contract · while the project exists</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Project tokens</dt>
			<dd>
				For a CI outside GitHub Actions, which has no GitHub token to publish with: kept as a hash, like the
				command line's.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Contract · until revoked</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Recordings</dt>
			<dd>
				What <code>versioncam publish</code> sends from your CI: each clip's timeline and the pictures of your
				app's screens it was recorded from; the commit, branch, run id and recorder version; the check's result
				and, when a clip broke, one picture of the page where it did. If the screens show personal data, you
				are its controller and we process it for you under the
				<a href="/legal/dpa/">data processing terms</a>.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">
				Contract; for what the screens show, your instructions · as your plan and the project's retention say:
				the daily sweep removes versions past them, and a branch's versions thirty days after its last publish
				(the default branch keeps its). Deleting a project removes its records at once and its files within
				minutes; the project's name stays as a tombstone, so a CI job cannot quietly bring it back
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Renders</dt>
			<dd>The videos and posters made from a recording.</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Contract · with their version</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>The record of the service</dt>
			<dd>
				Each publish's own record, for two hours; per account and month, counts of publishes, render seconds
				and bytes; and an event log per account: what kind of thing happened, when, and a line about it (a
				project's name, the reason for a suspension). For the plan's limits, the dashboard, support, and
				noticing abuse.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Contract and legitimate interest · usage by month; events 400 days, removed by the daily sweep</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Pull requests</dt>
			<dd>The number and head commit of each pull request the App reports on, for its check and its comment.</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Contract · with the project</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Request logs</dt>
			<dd>
				Cloudflare's log of each request to the service: method, address with its query string removed before
				it is written, status, timing and the country it came from. For operating and securing the service.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Legitimate interest · seven days (Cloudflare Workers Logs on the Workers Paid plan, checked {changed})</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>The services form</dt>
			<dd>
				Your name, an address to reply to, a company if you give one, what it is about and your message become
				one e-mail to us through Resend, with your address as the reply-to; the service stores none of it. Your
				IP address is counted for an hour (five messages an hour from one address), and everyone's messages
				together for a day (fifty).
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">
				Steps before a contract · the e-mail in our mailbox and in Resend's log; the hourly count an hour; the
				daily count 31 days
			</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Turnstile</dt>
			<dd>
				On the services form, Cloudflare judges whether a person is sending it. We learn pass or fail; what
				Cloudflare looks at in your browser is in its
				<a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/turnstile-privacy-policy/">Turnstile privacy addendum</a>.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Legitimate interest · the verdict is not kept</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Notification counts</dt>
			<dd>How many notification e-mails each project sent on each day, for the cap per project.</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Legitimate interest · 31 days</dd>
		</div>
		<div>
			<dt>Billing, once payment opens</dt>
			<dd>
				Your name, billing address and VAT number, given to Stripe at checkout, and the invoices Stripe makes.
				Your card goes to Stripe only and never reaches us.
			</dd>
			<dd class="vc-meta">Contract and legal obligation · invoices ten years</dd>
		</div>
	</dl>

	<h2>Where</h2>
	<p>
		Recordings, renders and the database are stored in Cloudflare's European Union jurisdiction. The pages and
		the API are served by Cloudflare's network, which answers from the location nearest you. A render holds a
		version's screens for the seconds it takes, where its container runs. E-mail is sent from Resend's EU
		region. GitHub is in the United States. Each company, what it does for us, and the basis on which personal
		data may leave the EU are on the <a href="/legal/subprocessors/">subprocessors page</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Who else sees what</h2>
	<p>
		GitHub receives the App's check runs, comments and issues on your repositories, on your project's
		instruction. Slack receives what your project's webhook says, if you gave it one. Nobody else: nothing is
		sold, shared or used for advertising, and there is no profiling.
	</p>

	<h2>On this website</h2>
	<p>
		No analytics, no tracking, and no third-party script, except Cloudflare's Turnstile on the services page.
		Fonts, clips and the badge on the front page come from version.cam's own hosts, so no request of yours leaves
		the site for anyone else.
	</p>

	<h2>Cookies</h2>
	<p>
		Two, both set by app.version.cam and readable by it alone: <code>__Host-vc_session</code> keeps you signed in
		for seven days, and <code>__Host-vc_oauth</code> lasts the ten minutes of signing in with GitHub. Both are
		strictly necessary (they are the sign-in), so no consent is asked and no banner is shown; the ePrivacy rules,
		as ZEKom-2 carries them in Slovenia, exempt storage a service cannot work without. version.cam sets no other
		cookie. The Turnstile widget on the services page is Cloudflare's, and its addendum says what it does in your
		browser.
	</p>

	<h2>Your rights</h2>
	<p>
		You may ask for a copy of your data, its correction, its deletion, a limit on its use, or its transfer to you
		in a usable form, and you may object to what we do on the basis of legitimate interest (GDPR, Articles 15 to
		21). Write to <a href={`mailto:${entity.privacyEmail}`}>{entity.privacyEmail}</a> from the e-mail address
		GitHub has for you, or from a confirmed notification address, so we know it is you. We answer within thirty
		days.
	</p>
	<p>
		Your own page on app.version.cam, reached from your login in the bar, has the two buttons: "Download my
		data" gives you everything above as one JSON file, and "Delete my account" removes your sessions, your
		command-line tokens, your memberships and access records, and your user record at once. An account that
		holds no projects, no subscription and no customer record goes with it; one that does stays as the
		customer's, and you are removed from it. A project you administer you delete yourself, in its settings, and
		you may download any version as a zip first. Either is also a request to the address above.
	</p>
	<p>
		You may complain to the Information Commissioner of the Republic of Slovenia (Informacijski pooblaščenec,
		Dunajska cesta 22, 1000 Ljubljana, <a href="https://www.ip-rs.si/">ip-rs.si</a>), or to the authority where
		you live.
	</p>

	<h2>Changes</h2>
	<p>
		This page was last changed on {changed}. A change that narrows what you may expect from us is sent to the
		e-mail address we have for your account thirty days before it applies. The version in force is always this
		page.
	</p>
</StarlightPage>
